CRITERION 7 - conforms to ACCESSIBILITY.md (table headers, contrast,
              focus states).  *** FAIL ***

  7a. WHAT PASSES.
    ACCESSIBILITY.md #2 (data tables use <th> with scope): all four headers
    carry scope="col" - conversion-sweep.html lines 47-50. Real <table>, no
    layout table.
    ACCESSIBILITY.md #3 (buttons act): the trigger is a real <button>
    (line 15), not a div.
    ACCESSIBILITY.md #8 (never colour alone): the current row pairs the
    indigo background with a literal "current" text badge (line 59); the
    surviving state pairs green with a check glyph and the word "survives"
    (line 64).
    ACCESSIBILITY.md #7/#12 for the table proper (computed with the WCAG
    relative-luminance formula):
      th text-gray-500 on white              4.83:1  pass
      th dark:text-gray-400 on gray-800      5.78:1  pass
      "survives" green-700 on white          5.02:1  pass
      lifetime tax red-600 on white          4.83:1  pass
      "current" badge indigo-700/indigo-100  6.41:1  pass

  7b. FAILURE - the focus-state and reduced-motion CSS does not exist in the
      shipped stylesheet. The template's a11y affordances are inert classes.
    $ make css-verify
      ./tmp/tailwindcss-3.4.17 -c tailwind.config.js -i web/static/css/tailwind.src.css -o tmp/tailwind.check.css --minify
      ERROR: web/static/css/tailwind.css is stale - run 'make css' and commit the result
      make: *** [Makefile:325: css-verify] Error 1
      CSSVERIFY_EXIT=2
    Attribution - the staleness is caused by THIS task, not inherited.
    Diffing the selector sets of the freshly built CSS against the committed
    one (python3 regex diff of tmp/tailwind.check.css vs
    web/static/css/tailwind.css) yields 7 added selectors and 0 removed:
      .focus\:outline:focus
      .focus\:outline-2:focus
      .focus\:outline-offset-2:focus
      .focus\:outline-indigo-500:focus
      .dark\:focus\:outline-indigo-400:focus:is(.dark ...)
      .motion-reduce\:animate-none
      .ring
    Every one of those class tokens appears in exactly one source file:
    $ grep -rn 'motion-reduce:animate-none' web/templates/ web/static/js/
      web/templates/components/whatif/conversion-sweep.html:23
    $ grep -rn 'focus:outline-indigo-500' web/templates/ web/static/js/
      web/templates/components/whatif/conversion-sweep.html:41
    (same single hit for focus:outline-2, focus:outline-offset-2,
     dark:focus:outline-indigo-400.)
    And the committed stylesheet has no rule for them:
    $ grep -c 'focus\\:outline-indigo-500' web/static/css/tailwind.css  -> 0
    $ grep -c 'motion-reduce'              web/static/css/tailwind.css  -> 0
    $ grep -c 'focus\\:outline-indigo-500' tmp/tailwind.check.css       -> 1
    $ grep -c 'motion-reduce'              tmp/tailwind.check.css       -> 1
    $ git status --porcelain web/static/css/tailwind.css                -> empty
      (the worker did not regenerate or commit it)

    Consequence, tied to numbered points:
    - ACCESSIBILITY.md #9 "focus indicator is visible". The results partial
      moves focus to a tabindex="-1" <h3> via
      hx-on::after-swap (conversion-sweep.html:19) - programmatic focus on a
      tabindex="-1" element does not reliably match :focus-visible, which is
      precisely why the worker added
      `focus:outline focus:outline-2 focus:outline-offset-2
       focus:outline-indigo-500 dark:focus:outline-indigo-400` at line 41.
      Those five classes resolve to nothing in the shipped CSS, so focus
      lands on an element with no visible indicator. The mechanism the
      template documents in its own comment ("with a visible focus ring",
      lines 35-38) is not delivered.
    - ACCESSIBILITY.md #15 "respects prefers-reduced-motion for any animated
      transition". The loading spinner is
      `class="animate-spin motion-reduce:animate-none"` (line 23).
      `animate-spin` IS in the committed CSS; `motion-reduce:animate-none` is
      NOT. The spinner therefore animates unconditionally, including under
      prefers-reduced-motion: reduce.
    - ACCESSIBILITY.md #10's focus-restoration intent is only half-delivered:
      the JS moves focus, but with no rendered indicator the restoration is
      invisible to a sighted keyboard user.
    Remedy: run `make css` and commit web/static/css/tailwind.css. No Go or
    template change is required. `make css-verify` must exit 0.

  7c. SECONDARY CONTRAST FINDING (reported, not the basis of the FAIL).
    The explanatory footnote at conversion-sweep.html:76 uses
    `text-xs text-gray-400 dark:text-gray-500`:
      gray-400 #9ca3af on white     -> 2.54:1  (ACCESSIBILITY.md #7 wants 4.5:1)
      gray-500 #6b7280 on gray-800  -> 3.04:1  (fails in dark mode too, #12)
    This is body text, not large text, so 4.5:1 applies. Mitigating context:
    this exact class pair is the established site-wide footnote convention -
    $ grep -rc 'text-gray-400 dark:text-gray-500' web/templates -r
      20 files, including rate-assumptions.html (18 uses) and the very file
      the spec told the worker to mirror, tax-optimizer.html:146 and :184.
    So the worker followed the mandated precedent; the defect is pre-existing
    and repo-wide, not introduced by this design. Flagging for the lead: if
    it is to be fixed it should be fixed as its own task across all 20 files,
    not silently here. Fixing 7b alone is sufficient to clear criterion 7 as
    the spec words it ("table headers, contrast, focus states") only if the
    lead accepts the site-wide footnote precedent; otherwise both must change.

  7d. OBSERVATION, no numbered point violated. The loading indicator span is
    aria-hidden="true" with no aria-live status text, so a screen-reader user
    gets no "running..." announcement while the sweep computes. ACCESSIBILITY
    .md #10 requires aria-live only for "destructive or state-changing
    actions"; a read-only sweep is neither. Not counted against the task.

